{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas","id":968703,"title":"Anthem frackas..","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-13T13:30:50-07:00","id":968703,"title":"Anthem frackas..","content":"Over the past 2 weeks or so, there has been a lot of furor over several football (and now even some other games players) either not standing for the national anthem being played, even ON 9/11, but in many cases doing other than just standing, such as 4 members of the Miami dolphins taking a knee like Colin Kaepernick did, or several of the Cleveland browns supposedly standing up and doing the black power/black panther raised fist salute.<br /> <br /> Now, i can agree that there is an issue with blacks and cops. However, there are those saying that &quot;he has the right to free speech and should not be forced to stand up etc, but the NFL said that one team couldn't use THEIR freedom of speech to honor cops via wearing a badge to honor the Dallas victims. So in that case, why is free speech being allowed if it is one way but not the other..<br /> <br /> Also, as someone who served, i take great pride in the Anthem and the flag, and see what is being done especially disrespectful, MORE so for those who did it on a national day of mourning to remember 9/11 victims.<br /> <br /> So what's everyone else's view point on this?<br /> <br /> For me, seeing Caepernick say this was all done to 'raise awareness of oppression (What oppression)' is a little off, especially when seeing him a multi-million dollar worth black man in a sport that is 68% Black, Compared to blacks only making up 13% of the US population (so they are well over represented). And if he was really concerned about struggles and poorness, why is he not using his many millions to HELP his fellow black man up?<br /> <br /> <br /> PS i asked Baroness Nerak and got the ok for this, AS LONG as we keep it civil...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11674,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","user":"garhkal","date":". 09-13-2016, 04:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T13:48:38-07:00","id":968719,"title":"","content":"It's no more disrespectful to not stand for the national anthem than it is to try and force a free citizen of this nation to behave how you want them to.<br /> <br /> And if this topic is only ok so long as it is kept civil, your comments about Caepernick should probably be revised, if not entirely retracted - you don't know what he does or doesn't do with his money, and there is no requirement that he has to throw money at a problem to prove that he is concerned about the problem, especially in this case because money isn't a viable solution.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not so noble anymore.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1823-drake","name":"Drake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1823_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"456"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T15:10:34-07:00","id":968766,"title":"","content":"Well, I think......<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas#post968703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">PS i asked Baroness Nerak and got the ok for this, AS LONG as we keep it civil...</div> </div> </div> </div>Nevermind, I'm really bad at that part.<br /> <br /> I'll just say this: He's exercising his <i>individual </i>rights as a free citizen to protest in a <i>peaceful </i>but very <i>public </i>way. A<i> whole team</i> trying to show support for another <i>organization </i>would fall under corporate/organization rules, not individual rights.<br /> <br /> And one man cannot be expected to solve the entire race-related poverty/pay discrimination by himself. Contrary to popular belief, throwing money at a problem rarely solves it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T15:39:57-07:00","id":968797,"title":"","content":"Not a fan of what Kaepernick and others are doing, but it's their prerogative. I have my own feelings as to why Kaepernick is doing this, but I figure I'll keep to them myself. <br /> <br /> Oh, and screw the 4 Dolphins players that decided to do it on 9-11. But...mostly because **** the Dolphins. : P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T19:32:59-07:00","id":968928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas#post968703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...but the NFL said that one team couldn't use THEIR freedom of speech to honor cops via wearing a badge to honor the Dallas victims. So in that case, why is free speech being allowed if it is one way but not the other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because the NFL is a corrupt institution that cares nothing for anything but profits. The NFL would let ISIS show propoganda films at games if the NFL thought it would make them more money than it would lose them.<br /> <br /> They don't care about values or principles. They don't care about their players' health. They don't care about holding their employees responsible for criminal activity.<br /> <br /> Seriously. This is an organization where doing pot is worse for your career than sexual assault. Expecting anything but hypocrisy from the NFL is naive.<br /> <br /> Also, remember that the NFL, despite billionaire owners and millionaire players? It's a <b>non-profit</b> with an exception written just for football that no other sport can use.<br /> <br /> So don't look to, the NF-how is a slur against Native Americans racist-L for principled or consistent actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So what's everyone else's view point on this? </div> </div> </div>I think people should be offended. But then they should look at why they're getting offended and put their energy to doing the right thing: making our country better because someone's getting people to actually talk about a problem that needs fixing instead of just ignoring it as soon as possible when it rears its ugly head again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, seeing Caepernick say this was all done to 'raise awareness of oppression (What oppression)' is a little off, especially when seeing him a multi-million dollar worth black man in a sport that is 68% Black, Compared to blacks only making up 13% of the US population (so they are well over represented). And if he was really concerned about struggles and poorness, why is he not using his many millions to HELP his fellow black man up? </div> </div> </div>Kaepernick was born to a single teenaged mother in Milwaukee who had to give him up for adoption because she was too poor to raise him. So he got lucky sure, but in a way nobody should have to be. From being born to a woman so poor she had to give up her child, he managed to make it in an incredibly harsh industry. That's generally what we call &quot;the American Dream&quot; not &quot;what is this guy whining about.&quot;<br /> <br /> Also he doesn give his money to charity, and has promised to explicitly give all the money he's making off his jersey (which is currently the best selling jersey in the NFL right now... though a lot of them are being burned), to charity.<br /> <br /> So yeah, he's walking what he's talking. Even if you disagree with his choices, he's exactly what we keep saying this country stands for. He is what you served to protect.<br /> <br /> So I say save your outrage for all the rich people that don't use their money for anything positive for anyone else, or the one's that only use their fame for negativity. I doubt that's why you felt it was important to go to war for the rest of us.<br /> <br /> -------<br /> <br /> Also, the whole &quot;but the NFL is so black!&quot; thing... please, don't do that. Black people aren't so prevalent in the NFL because of some sort of anti-non-black racism, or something. It's because sports is one of the few avenues to success most black men have if they didn't get a good start in life. The NFL will take you if you got screwed by our education system. The NFL will take you if you got screwed by our criminal justice system. Etc. Which are problems that disproportionately impact black communities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 09-14-2016, 12:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T20:05:32-07:00","id":968951,"title":"","content":"The NFL being an organization with issues is a non-sequitur so regardless of what it does that is or might seem hypocritical, it doesn't detract from what is being protested about. Second, not only did Kaepernick donate money, but the 49ers organization did as well, so questioning what he is doing with his money is not only not accurate but also non-sequitur. Frankly, as a veteran, I support Kaepernick, BLM,etc. The fundamental nature of American society, and Western civilization in general, is just a LITTLE more important to me than the sour grapes of nationalistic zeal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T20:06:38-07:00","id":968952,"title":"","content":"Folks sure put a lot of energy into being angry about black people protesting. Instead of being upset that someone is being rude to a <i>goddamn flag</i>, maybe look at why this keeps coming up and ask why you're getting so angry about it. There are bigger problems out there.<br /> <br /> Truly loving your country means acknowledging that it isn't perfect and trying to change it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T21:55:55-07:00","id":969011,"title":"","content":"It did what it was designed to do, generate controversy and visibility. That's how protest works. <br /> <br /> All I've seen by way of rebuttal is a bunch of goalpost moving (pun partially intended). Attacking how much money he makes. Attacking his history or his character. Making false comparisons to other people supposedly more worthy than him. Attacking whether the protest is &quot;appropriate.&quot; All of which dodges the fundamental question: does one think that black people are victims of oppression in this country? One can quibble degrees and definitions if they want, but that's still dodging the question. At some level either you do or you don't. Saying that you do but don't approve of the protest is another way of saying you don't believe the oppression is real because it is effectively saying that the value of those being oppressed is less than the value of a symbol. <br /> <br /> You take everything else away, and it comes down to a yes or no answer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T22:30:14-07:00","id":969026,"title":"","content":"People are free to do what they want, but they have realize that there will be blowback. Someone refusing to respect the nation might find himself or herself losing endorsement contracts or attracting bad publicity. In this case, if he's willing to take all that for his beliefs, good on him.<br /> <br /> All that said, rejecting America's traditions and symbols is probably not a good way to get Americans to rally around your cause and support you. More likely many will have the attitude of, &quot;If you're rejecting America then why should I (and other Americans) care what happens to you?&quot; That's not necessarily an attitude you want to instill in other people if you're looking to receive their support for long-term social changes. This was one of the problems the Hippies and anti-war protesters in the 60's and 70's ran into. Their overt rejection of America's symbols (including flag burning) only ended with many members of mainstream America rejecting their opinions and stances out of hand.<br /> <br /> I suspect that if many other African-Americans begin rejecting the symbol of America like that, you'll see similar attitudes of, &quot;If African-Americans hate America so much then I don't care what happens to them,&quot; which is pretty much the absolute <i>opposite</i> of the goal that African-Americans who want change in the social, political and justice systems of America are seeking. They <i>need</i> majority white America on their side.<br /> <br /> Honestly I think if you want to affect social change, you're better off draping yourself in the flag and using it to appeal to the sense of brotherhood and solidarity with other Americans than you are with rejecting it and of American symbols. &quot;We're American just like you, we love this country just like you, and as Americans you have a duty to stand with us to prevent the abuse of your fellow citizens at the hands of our shared government.&quot; That would probably work better to get Americans on your side, I would think.<br /> <br /> In the end, publicly and loudly refuse to stand for the anthem (or otherwise rejecting national symbols) can send a powerful message, but the way many people will interpret and respond to that message might be completely counter the goals that the individual had in mind when he refused to stand for the anthem. I'll agree that it's not right or fair or just that a lot of people will react to that message in that way, but well, the fact is, that's how a lot of people are going to react to it.<br /> <br /> I think that there may be better ways of getting white American on board with the idea that African-Americans face serious social and judicial discrimination and that this needs to be changed, than doing things that will make white America pull away out of some twisted sense of patriotic obligation to reject the idea that those changes need to be made.<br /> <br /> Well, this largely comes back to arguments among African-Americans during the fights against racial segregation - there was a lot of debate and discussion in the NAACP and other organizations as to how the protests should look. Martin Luther King Jr. and many other leaders felt that the best way to affect change was to bring white Americans on board and create a sense of solidarity by reminding them how injustice towards African-Americans was injustice towards <i>all</i> Americans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 09-14-2016, 01:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T23:20:21-07:00","id":969037,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drake</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=968719#post968719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's no more disrespectful to not stand for the national anthem than it is to try and force a free citizen of this nation to behave how you want them to.<br /> <br /> And if this topic is only ok so long as it is kept civil, your comments about Caepernick should probably be revised, if not entirely retracted - you don't know what he does or doesn't do with his money, and there is no requirement that he has to throw money at a problem to prove that he is concerned about the problem, especially in this case because money isn't a viable solution.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is true, i don't know for a fact how much he has given to charity, but looking it up, i see a few articles from the beginning of Sept saying he will give a mil to charity, but not much else other than him giving time to a youth organization.. And that charity donation he is looking to give over, came after this hoopla started..<br /> <br /> As for the whole 'its no more disrespectful', its not trying to force him to 'act as i want to', to ask someone to respect the anthem.. Heck most states have flag laws in place.. do you consider them equally wrong?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=968766#post968766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I think......<br /> <br /> Nevermind, I'm really bad at that part.<br /> <br /> I'll just say this: He's exercising his <i>individual </i>rights as a free citizen to protest in a <i>peaceful </i>but very <i>public </i>way. A<i> whole team</i> trying to show support for another <i>organization </i>would fall under corporate/organization rules, not individual rights.</div> </div> </div> </div>Individual rights on his own time.. But when in uniform he is representing the org. Much like as a member of the military i have all the right in the world to go to political rallies OFF duty, but can't when in uniform. UNLESS its an official duty (like i am doing honor guard detail or band)..<br /> Its similar to how if i worked for Coco-cola, lets say, when in uniform i can't be seen drinking Pepsi products or the like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=968766#post968766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And one man cannot be expected to solve the entire race-related poverty/pay discrimination by himself. Contrary to popular belief, throwing money at a problem rarely solves it.</div> </div> </div> </div>True dat.. Look at how much we have spent on both the 'war on poverty and the war on drugs', yet more people are in poverty now than before..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=968928#post968928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Also, the whole &quot;but the NFL is so black!&quot; thing... please, don't do that. Black people aren't so prevalent in the NFL because of some sort of anti-non-black racism, or something. It's because sports is one of the few avenues to success most black men have if they didn't get a good start in life. The NFL will take you if you got screwed by our education system. The NFL will take you if you got screwed by our criminal justice system. Etc. Which are problems that disproportionately impact black communities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I brought that up, to aruge the point that if he feels blacks are still being oppressed, then how is it in 3 of our main sports, blacks are more represented than any other ethnicity... NFL, NBA and track/field (mostly in the shorter race ranges)..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=968952#post968952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Folks sure put a lot of energy into being angry about black people protesting. Instead of being upset that someone is being rude to a <i>goddamn flag</i>, maybe look at why this keeps coming up and ask why you're getting so angry about it. There are bigger problems out there.<br /> <br /> Truly loving your country means acknowledging that it isn't perfect and trying to change it.</div> </div> </div> </div>To members of the military (and their families) its not just a 'goddamn flag' though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T23:40:09-07:00","id":969047,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11674_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11674\" data-vbnamecard=\"11674\">garhkal</a> My father is a decorated Navy veteran, but thanks for assuming.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-14T00:35:57-07:00","id":969070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=969037#post969037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the whole 'its no more disrespectful', its not trying to force him to 'act as i want to', to ask someone to respect the anthem..</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Fuck the National Anthem.<br /> And the Pledge of Allegiance.<br /> And the President.<br /> And the Declaration of Independence.<br /> The entire Armed forces can sit on my dick.<br /> All the Judicial, Legislative and Executive branches too.<br /> <br /> Because all of that, every piece of that is absolutely fucking nothing compared to the thing they're supposed to safeguard. Our Rights.<br /> All else is meaningless if we disregard those. If we demand our fellow citizens to disregard those. If we gainsay those rights because, today is the special &quot;NEVER FORGET! BECAUSE WE CAN'T CONTROL YOU IF YOU CAN'T!&quot; day, then we've given up our liberties as surely as all those victims gave up their lives. <br /> <br /> But worse, because we'd be doing it by choice","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-14T00:46:16-07:00","id":969074,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=969026#post969026\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, this largely comes back to arguments among African-Americans during the fights against racial segregation - there was a lot of debate and discussion in the NAACP and other organizations as to how the protests should look. Martin Luther King Jr. and many other leaders felt that the best way to affect change was to bring white Americans on board and create a sense of solidarity by reminding them how injustice towards African-Americans was injustice towards <i>all</i> Americans.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that the methods of then aren't necessarily the most effective methods of today.<br /> <br /> The &quot;respectability politics&quot; worked really well against the overt racism of that era, but has proven to be stunningly ineffective and moving the ball forwards on institutional and similarly subtle racism. Decades of this approach have not solved the issue of massive prejudice in police departments (which the DoJ finds every time they end up investigating one). Sometimes the politics of disruption are what's called for.<br /> <br /> As well, you point towards the hippies as &quot;failing&quot; which was true in the short term, but in the long run a lot of the social movements attached to the hippies have become the &quot;norm&quot; of America despite the initial rejections by mainstream society. The battle is not always convincing the people in power now, but influencing how the people in power in the future will think about these topics.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/968703-anthem-frackas?p=969037#post969037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Heck most states have flag laws in place.. do you consider them equally wrong?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, and they're largely considered unconstitutional even if there's no attempt to get them to the Supreme Court to make that the case.<br /> <br /> If various flag laws (including the federal one) were actually taken to the Supreme Court, they'd have to deal with the current precedent that says enshrining national symbols in the law like that is equivalent to establishing a state religion, and it violates the freedom of religion for religions that refuse to make such displays to secular symbols (ex: the Jehovah's Witnesses); before even getting into the freedom of speech part of it.<br /> <br /> As such laws are entirely symbolic (they carry no legal penalties, they're laws is no consequences for breaking).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Individual rights on his own time.. But when in uniform he is representing the org. Much like as a member of the military i have all the right in the world to go to political rallies OFF duty, but can't when in uniform. </div> </div> </div>Erm... no. Corporate uniforms are not the same as military uniforms in significance and how people wearing them should be expected to act. This is even enshrined in the law. Even if a corporation wants to make it policy that you can't do X while wearing a corporate uniform, it can't hold up in court because that isn't allowed to trump your constitutional rights. Joining the military however, includes relinquishing your full constitutional rights in favor of the UCMJ.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its similar to how if i worked for Coco-cola, lets say, when in uniform i can't be seen drinking Pepsi products or the like. </div> </div> </div>Unless you're actively promoting a competing product... yes you can.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> True dat.. Look at how much we have spent on both the 'war on poverty and the war on drugs', yet more people are in poverty now than before.. </div> </div> </div>These are two very different things. The war on drugs was a pointless boondoggle. The war on poverty was something that had a potential to work, but feel quickly to a number of issues; including a lack of adequate funding to essential programs. There's a lot of poverty reducing programs out there that did work, but don't work now because they've been gutted into a joke, while less effective ones are still getting funding for various reasons. The war on poverty could work, if done more effectively, and with proper funding.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I brought that up, to aruge the point that if he feels blacks are still being oppressed, then how is it in 3 of our main sports, blacks are more represented than any other ethnicity... NFL, NBA and track/field (mostly in the shorter race ranges).. </div> </div> </div>Because a small minority of blacks having jobs in sports doesn't stop the vast majority black people from being discriminated against in other aspects of life. Being upper-middle class or better doesn't even protect many black men from feeling discriminated against. Lots of well off blacks consider being pulled over for driving while black, a regular occurrence.<br /> <br /> And I'm trying to be respectful here, but this is aping the arguments of every reactionary attempt to push back against actual equality. &quot;How can the blacks feel oppressed after we abolished slavery?&quot; &quot;How can blacks still feel oppressed now that they can vote?&quot; &quot;How can they still feel oppressed now that we've outlawed poll taxes and housing discrimination, seriously what more do they want?&quot; &quot;How can blacks still feel oppressed with lots of famous and power black people on TV.... doesn't one rich football player make up for 1000 black men screwed by the criminal justice system?&quot;<br /> <br /> It all really boils down to one real question that you're asking, &quot;Why can't black people be satisfied with what they have and stop actually wanting to be equals with whites?&quot;<br /> <br /> Because that's what this whole, &quot;but black people dominate sports!&quot; really comes down to.<br /> <br /> [quoteTo members of the military (and their families) its not just a 'goddamn flag' though.[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> To some members. You don't speak for all current service members, for all vets, or for all families. There's plenty of active members and vets that are in full support of at least his right to protest as he sees fit within the bounds of the law, that don't feel slighted because of a symbolic gesture being protested with a different symbolic gesture.<br /> <br /> Because whatever the flag means to you, that's all it is. A symbol. Standing at the national anthem is just a symbolic gesture. It's an important and meaningful one, but making a sacred one is ignoring what it supposedly stands for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-14T01:03:35-07:00","id":969079,"title":"","content":"AND ITS FUCKING SPELLED FRACAS! FRACKAS IS NOT A GODDAMNED WORD!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","ava